A forgotten topological index\n  * A unified approach to the extremal trees for different indices\n  * Bounds for Zagreb indices\n  * Each (n,m)-graph having the i-th minimal Laplacian coefficient is a threshold graph\n  * Extremal unicyclic and bicyclic graphs with respect to the \\(F\\)-index\n  * Graphs with smallest forgotten index\n  * Laplacian spectra and spanning trees of threshold graphs\n  * Lower and upper bounds of the forgotten topological index\n  * On extremal trees with respect to the F-index\n  * On molecular graphs with smallest and greatest zeroth-order general Randić index\n  * On the forgotten topological index\n  * On the forgotten topological index of graphs\n  * Some inequalities for the first general Zagreb index of graphs and line graphs\n  * Some new inequalities for the forgotten topological index and coindex of graphs\n  * Some properties of the first general Zagreb index\n  * Sum of powers of the degrees of graphs: extremal results and bounds\n  * Sum of squares of degrees in a graph\n  * Threshold graphs and related topics\n  * F-index of some graph operations\n\nThis page was built for publication: On extremal connected graphs with respect to the forgotten topological index--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Laplacian and Signless Laplacian Permanental Polynomials of Some Well-known Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14389)\nciteturn5academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; We study two fundamental variants: the Laplacian permanental polynomial $\\psi(L(G);x)$ and signless Laplacian permanental polynomial $\\psi(Q(G);x)$ of a graph $G$. ... This paper investigates the permanental characterization problem for several families of starlike graphs, including: spider graphs (tree), coconut tree, perfect binary tree, corona product of $C_m$ and $K_n$, and $\\bar K_n$ for various values of $m$ and $n$.\nTitle: On Laplacian and Signless Laplacian Permanental Polynomials of Some Well-known Graphs\nAuthors: Sarbari Mitra, Soumya Bhoumik\nDate: Wed Sep 17 19:47:35 2025\n\nThe permanent of an $n \\times n$ matrix $M = (m_{ij})$ is defined as $\\mathrm{per}(M) = \\sum_{\\sigma \\in S_n} \\prod_{i=1}^n m_{i,\\sigma(i)}$, where $S_n$ denotes the symmetric group on $\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}$. The permanental polynomial of $M$, is defined by $\\psi(M;x) = \\mathrm{per}(xI_n - M)$. We study two fundamental variants: the Laplacian permanental polynomial $\\psi(L(G);x)$ and signless Laplacian permanental polynomial $\\psi(Q(G);x)$ of a graph $G$. A graph is said to be {determined} by its (signless) Laplacian permanental polynomial if no other non-isomorphic graph shares the same polynomial. A graph is combinedly determined when isomorphism is guaranteed by the equality of both polynomials. Characterizing which graphs are determined by their(signless) Laplacian permanental polynomials is an interesting problem. This paper investigates the permanental characterization problem for several families of starlike graphs, including: spider graphs (tree), coconut tree, perfect binary tree, corona product of $C_m$ and $K_n$, and $\\bar K_n$ for various values of $m$ and $n$. We establish which of these graphs are determined by their Laplacian or signless Laplacian permanental polynomials, and which require both polynomials for complete characterization. We emphasize that in this manuscript, we have considered a few techniques to compute the permanental polynomial of matrices and their propagation.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMatch, Volume 95, No.1 (2026) – DOI UBKG (https://doi.ub.kg.ac.rs/doi/casopisi/10-46793-match95-1/)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Zhang: On Extremal Connected Graphs with Respect to the Forgotten Topological Index, pp. ... Rada: Local Value of a Vertex-Degree Function Index of a Graph, pp.\n\n# Match, Volume 95, No.1 (2026)\n\n  * MatchVolume 95, No.1 (2026)Часописи\