e number of edges in a minimally |$(d+1)$|-connected graph |$G = (V,E)$| on at least |$3d+1$| vertices:\n\n  * (P4)\n\n|$|E| \\leq (d+1)n - {(d+1)}^{2}$|⁠.\n\nWe conjecture that |$d$|-stress-independent graphs also satisfy (P4), provided that the number of vertices is large enough (Conjecture 4.5). For |$d=1$|⁠, this follows from the observation that (P3) implies (P4) for graphs on at least |$4$| vertices. We will verify the |$d=2$| case of this conjecture for graphs on at least |$8$| vertices (Theorem 4.6).\n\nWe also consider the family of minimally |$\\mathcal {R}_{d}$|-connected graphs—graphs whose |$d$|-dimensional rigidity matroid is connected, but does not remain so after the deletion of any edge. This family was investigated by the third author in [13], where it was conjectured (and verified when |$d=2$|⁠) that (P1) holds for minimally |$\\mathcal {R}_{d}$|-connected graphs with |$n \\geq d+2$|⁠, and that (P4) holds when |$n \\geq \\binom{d+2}{2}+1$|⁠. We partly resolve this conjecture by showing that (P2) holds for minimally |$\\mathcal {R}_{d}$|-connected graphs for all |$n$| and |$d$|⁠, and that (P3) holds when |$n\\geq d+2$|⁠. In fact, we prove a slightly stronger result that also includes the case of minimally redundantly |$d$|-rigid graphs (see Theorem 5.2). In addition, we show that a conjecture from [3] would imply that every minimally |$\\mathcal {R}_{d}$|-connected graph is |$d$|-stress-independent (Proposition 5.6). See Figure 1 for an overview of the graph classes considered in this paper and our main results and conjectures.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) On Laplacian and Signless Laplacian Permanental Polynomials of Some Well-known Graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395648888_On_Laplacian_and_Signless_Laplacian_Permanental_Polynomials_of_Some_Well-known_Graphs)\nciteturn5search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; its (signless) Laplacian pe